Hmm... Your suggestion sounds a lot like Omega. I don't think you can
get a boat, but there are several dungeons, a wilderness area, and a large
city. I think the problem with this sort of game is the time required to
play. In NetHack and Rogue, you can start out immediately. There is no
huge amount of time involved in getting equipment. In Moria, there is
some initial tediousness with the shopkeepers (You have to haggle about
prices. Kind of dull...), but once you are outfitted it returns to a 
Rogue sort of hack and slay game. There are more special items and a 
better magic system, though...

Omega takes ages to just get equiped. VERY BORING! I have been playing on and
off and have yet to survive a few feet out of the city. The nice thing
about it is that not everything is simple. There doesn't seem to be a 
very clear cut Goal, as there is in NetHack. This allows for more discovery,
something I always felt NetHack lacked. The greatest mystery I ever faced
in NetHack was which weapon did more damage :-)

What would be nice is some combination of the two. Maybe a small town to get
equiped in, NOT a city, and a few dungeons in the country. Some hidden 
surprises can always be nice, like interesting magic devices. I would
also like to see monsters that USE the treasure they are carrying! That
might make the game more challenging. A different sort of magic system would
also be nice. All the games I have played use spellbooks and mana. 
How about a more intricate system? Or at least a different one? Don't stick
with the old NetHack one. It's much too simple. Try looking at some other
role playing games. Not all of these things have to be based on D&D. I
think the major problem with most of the adventure games out there is they're
sticking to the norm. Do something different! It would be refreshing.

